---
import CaseStudyFigure from "./CaseStudyFigure.astro";
import "../styles/case-study.css";
import originalSearch from "../../../../assets/figma-exports/USA Spending Search_final.png";

const source = "Original interface design.";
const feedbackUrl = "https://onevoicecrm.my.site.com/usaspending/s/question/0D53d000005SK2HCAW/look-and-feel-of-search-page";

// Crop coordinates use a half-scale reference of the original 1520px-wide export.
function searchCrop(alt: string, x: number, y: number, width: number, height: number) {
  return { src: originalSearch.src, width: originalSearch.width / 2, alt, crop: { x, y, width, height } };
}
---

<article class="case-study">
  <header class="case-opening">
    <p class="case-label">USAspending.gov · Search Redesign</p>
    <h1>From finding records to understanding federal spending</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">I redesigned USAspending’s Search page to help people investigate where federal money goes. Filters, detailed records, charts and maps worked together so users could refine a question and explore the results.</p>
    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>Primary designer</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Search, filtering and data analysis</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Launch</dt><dd>2017, with analytical views added over time</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <dl class="case-facts case-highlights">
      <div><dt>Refine searches in context</dt><dd>Keep filters beside the results they affect.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Explore spending in different ways</dt><dd>Switch between records, time trends, maps and category comparisons.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Find relevant industry codes</dt><dd>Search and browse classifications without needing to know every code.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <figure class="case-feedback">
      <blockquote>“Just wanted to say how much better the search page is now.”</blockquote>
      <figcaption>Fransi Dunagan · USAspending Community · December 2017<br /><a href={feedbackUrl}>Read the original feedback</a></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </header>

  <div class="case-lead-figure">
    <CaseStudyFigure size="large" caption="Final USAspending Search interface design" source={source}
      image={searchCrop("USAspending Search design with filters on the left and award records on the right.", 0, 0, 760, 640)} />
  </div>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>USAspending makes federal spending data available to the public. The previous Search page focused on finding individual records through forms and tables.</p>
      <p>The redesign aimed to support broader questions: How much did an agency spend? Who received funding? Where did the money go? Users needed ways to explore these questions as well as look up specific records.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>My Role and Collaboration</h2>
      <p>I designed the layout, filters, tables, maps and analytical views, from early wireframes through final designs and engineering specifications.</p>
      <p>I worked with product owners, researchers, subject matter experts and engineers to connect user needs with what the data could support.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Design Process</h2>
      <h3>Research</h3>
      <p>I reviewed the existing site and other data-analysis tools. Subject matter experts helped me understand the spending measures, record structures and data limitations that would shape the design.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseStudyFigure size="small" caption="Research, collaborative exploration and user feedback informed the designs prepared for engineering." source="Reconstructed from the project process.">
      <ol class="case-diagram">
        <li><strong>Research</strong><span>Legacy review and subject matter expert input</span></li>
        <li><strong>Explore and refine</strong><span>Team sketches, wireframes and open beta feedback</span></li>
        <li><strong>Prepare for rollout</strong><span>Specifications, engineering handoff and phased release</span></li>
      </ol>
    </CaseStudyFigure>
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h3>Initial Design Round</h3>
      <p>Team sketching sessions helped us explore layouts and agree on a direction. I developed wireframes connecting filters, spending summaries, charts, maps and a configurable results table.</p>

      <h3>Feedback and Refinement</h3>
      <p>We shared early concepts through an open beta and invited existing users to comment. Confusion around terminology and specialized filters reinforced the need for explanations and browsable classifications.</p>

      <h3>Engineering Handoff and Rollout</h3>
      <p>I delivered detailed designs, specifications and interaction demonstrations, then supported engineering during implementation. Technical constraints required a table-first launch, with analytical views added in later releases.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section case-decisions">
    <div class="case-prose"><h2>Key Design Decisions</h2></div>

    <section class="case-decision">
      <div class="case-prose">
        <h3>01 / Keep filters and results together</h3>
        <p>Searching often involved reviewing results, adjusting filters and trying again. I placed filters in a sidebar beside the results so users could repeat that process without opening a form over their work.</p>
      <p>Visible filter selections kept the current query available for review.</p>
      <p>The sidebar left less room for results, but I prioritized keeping search controls accessible during repeated refinement.</p>
      </div>
      <CaseStudyFigure size="large" caption="Filters remain beside results so users can refine a search while reviewing what it returns." source={source}
        image={searchCrop("Filter sidebar and spending results within the same workspace.", 26, 112, 710, 518)} />
    </section>

    <section class="case-decision">
      <div class="case-prose">
        <h3>02 / Make complex filters easier to use</h3>
        <p>Some filters required knowledge of federal spending terminology. I kept familiar terms for experienced users and added explanations to help others understand their options.</p>
      <p>For industry classifications, I combined keyword search with a browsable hierarchy. Users could find a known code, see its broader category and discover related codes they might otherwise miss.</p>
      </div>
      <CaseStudyFigure size="large" caption="Keyword matches appear within the industry hierarchy, helping users discover relevant codes and understand their broader categories.">
        <div class="case-annotation">
          <div class="case-placeholder">NAICS filter screenshot</div>
          <div>
            <h4>Searching codes in the context of their hierarchy</h4>
            <dl>
              <div><dt>Find relevant matches</dt><dd>Keyword matching highlights related labels, supporting discovery without requiring an exact industry code.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Understand broader categories</dt><dd>The visible parent chain helps users recognize broader classifications that may fit their question.</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Explore related branches</dt><dd>Matching terms appear across branches, revealing other classifications that may be relevant to the search.</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </CaseStudyFigure>
      <div class="case-prose">
        <p>Fiscal-year shortcuts supported common time selections, while a date-range option allowed more specific searches. Clear labels, search suggestions and visible selections helped users keep track of their choices.</p>
      </div>
      <CaseStudyFigure size="medium" caption="Fiscal-year shortcuts support common selections, while a date-range option allows more specific periods." source={source}
        image={searchCrop("Time Period filter with fiscal-year shortcuts and a Date Range tab.", 30, 222, 166, 162)} />
      <div class="case-figure-pair">
        <CaseStudyFigure size="medium" caption="A help entry point beside the keyword control makes guidance available where users begin their search.">
          <div class="case-placeholder">Keyword search with help entry point</div>
        </CaseStudyFigure>
        <CaseStudyFigure size="medium" caption="Visible selections keep the current search criteria available for review and adjustment." source={source}
          image={searchCrop("Current Filters area listing fiscal years and award type selections.", 206, 116, 531, 63)} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="case-decision">
      <div class="case-prose">
        <h3>03 / Connect detailed records with broader analysis</h3>
        <p>A table helped users inspect individual records. Charts and maps helped them investigate patterns across time, locations and spending categories.</p>
      <p>I designed these views around the same filtered results, allowing users to explore the data without first downloading it and building their own charts. Configurable table columns also let users bring relevant fields into view.</p>
      </div>
      <CaseStudyFigure size="large" caption="A sortable table supports detailed inspection, with additional columns available for different analytical needs." source={source}
        image={searchCrop("Sortable results table with award type tabs and additional columns.", 211, 189, 520, 448)} />
      <CaseStudyFigure size="medium" caption="Charts and maps provide complementary ways to examine patterns within the filtered results." source={source}>
        <div class="case-analysis-pair">
          <div class="case-analysis-crop time"><img src={originalSearch.src} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Spending Over Time bar chart." /></div>
          <div class="case-analysis-crop map"><img src={originalSearch.src} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Spending by Geography map of the United States." /></div>
        </div>
      </CaseStudyFigure>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Evaluation and Outcome</h2>
      <p>The redesigned Search page brought filtering, records and visual analysis into one public workspace. It supported both specific lookups and broader spending investigations.</p>
      <p>Community feedback praised the organization of search options and the ability to move between results and graphs. This provided positive feedback on the experience, though we did not establish a measured improvement in search speed or accuracy.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseStudyFigure size="small" caption="The initial release prioritized filters and table-based results, while the broader design guided the addition of analytical views." source="Reconstructed from the project process.">
      <ol class="case-diagram case-diagram--two">
        <li><strong>Initial release</strong><span>Filters and detailed records table</span></li>
        <li><strong>Later releases</strong><span>Time, geographic and category views</span></li>
      </ol>
    </CaseStudyFigure>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>Reflections &amp; Lessons Learned</h2>
      <h3>Powerful tools need guidance</h3>
      <p>Novice users searching for construction spending in Virginia often entered both terms as keywords. Location filters and industry codes could define the relevant records more precisely, but users needed help recognizing when to use them.</p>
      <p>I learned that offering detailed controls also means explaining how they support a person’s question.</p>

      <h3>Explain limits when they affect the user</h3>
      <p>A contract labeled “office supplies” could contain a transaction for “pencils” without appearing in a keyword search for pencils. A separate transaction search supported that deeper investigation.</p>
      <p>Downloads could also take time to prepare. I designed a status tab to explain the wait and provide the file when ready. These cases reinforced the need to make system behavior understandable.</p>

      <h3>Keep delivery stages clear</h3>
      <p>The table-first launch taught me to separate the intended experience from what could ship immediately. Working with engineering and the client helped us define an initial release while keeping a shared direction for later improvements.</p>
    </div>
    <CaseStudyFigure size="small" caption="A term found within a transaction may be absent from its parent contract record, making the level of search important to discovery." source="Reconstructed explanatory example.">
      <ul class="case-diagram case-diagram--two">
        <li><strong>Contract record</strong><span>Office supplies</span></li>
        <li><strong>Transaction description</strong><span>Pencils</span></li>
      </ul>
    </CaseStudyFigure>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-prose">
      <h2>What I’d Refine Today</h2>
      <h3>Offer useful starting points</h3>
      <p>Add example searches and short tutorials showing how filters work together.</p>

      <h3>Create a clearer overview</h3>
      <p>Bring key totals and related charts closer together so users can assess their results before exploring individual records.</p>

      <h3>Support time comparisons</h3>
      <p>Help users compare spending across periods, with clear labels explaining the dates and spending measures involved.</p>
      <p class="case-disclosure">AI-assisted tools helped organize and edit this case study. The work, decisions and recollections described are my own.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <footer class="case-closing">
    <a href="/work">← Back to selected work</a>
    <a href="/contact">Let’s talk</a>
  </footer>
</article>
